Lithurgy

Ingredients: POST-ROCK + RAW, GRITTY & BRUTALIST + Bass
Creative Catalyst: It grew out of a monastery where monks transcribe dreams into modular synth patches.

Lithurgy sounds like seismic mass being chanted through reinforced concrete. It discards the glittering, delay-soaked guitars typical of post-rock, constructing instead gargantuan, non-resolving architectural movements led entirely by heavily fuzzed electric basses, bowed contrabass, and unpredictable, sleep-derived modular CV bursts. The sonic space feels cavernous yet suffocatingly raw—an ecclesiastical tectonic tremor that rattles listeners in their marrow.

Genre DNA
* Tempo & Pulse: Glacial and non-metronomic, hovering between 44 and 68 BPM. The pulse behaves like shifting tectonic plates or slow monastic breathing rather than a traditional drum groove, anchored by dragging foot-stomp transients and subterranean bass pulses.
* Harmonic Palette: Dorian and Phrygian modal chants transposed down into bone-shaking low registers; unyielding drone roots (often drop-tuned to C0 or A-1) clashing against harsh, microtonal modular feedback intervals.
* Production Signatures: 
  1. Sub-Audible Architecture Capture: Amplifiers are deliberately positioned in poured-concrete cisterns and subterranean tunnels, mic'd from hundreds of feet away to capture natural slapback and structural low-end resonance.
  2. Somnographic Ribbon Curing: An impossible, arcane process where monks run magnetic tape through wet slurry troughs of drying Portland cement while transcribing alpha-wave sleep voltages directly into modular synthesizer control voltages, printing the physical silt and dream jitter directly onto the magnetic oxide.
  3. Unfiltered DI Rupture: Overdriven bass guitars bypass traditional preamps to clip directly into transformerless mixer channels, leaving grit, string buzz, and mechanical fret clatter completely unvarnished.
* Dynamics: Instead of the formulaic "quiet-to-explosive" crescendo of traditional post-rock, tracks operate like sustained stress-loads on an overpass. They open with abrasive, low-frequency hums that accumulate physical density, holding audiences inside an excruciating, monolithic plateau before shearing off abruptly into room decay.

Scene & Origin Lore
Lithurgy originated within the Abbey of Our Lady of the Poured Slab, an austere 1963 brutalist monastery carved into the slate crags of the Swiss Jura mountains. Founded by a splinter cell of Benedictines who believed spoken prayer was corrupted by mortal ego, the monks maintained strict silence, communicating only through voltages. Each brother slept wearing hand-wound encephalogram ribbons connected to an enormous, wall-mounted Eurorack modular matrix in the nave. What began as private spiritual meditation mutated when local alpine squatters and industrial dropouts were drawn to the low-frequency vibrations that shook the valley floor during night vigils. A reclusive subculture formed around the compound: youth dressed in rough-spun woolen cowl-smocks worn over steel-toed demolition gear, carrying unshielded patch cords as prayer beads. Gatherings, known as "Pours," are held in shuttered cement plants and damp bunkers where no one speaks, eyes remain closed, and crowds stand motionless, allowing room-rattling bass drones to physically displace their ribcages.

Typical Instruments
* Primary Bass Force: Down-tuned 6-string electric bass guitars (tuned to octave-down Drop A), processed through customized industrial fuzz boxes and played with horsehair cellos bows; electric contrabass; dual bass synthesizers (Moog Taurus-style bass pedals).
* Electronics & Synthesis: Custom Eurorack modular systems driven by bio-voltage and sleep-telemetry controllers; Serge wave multipliers; Buchla-style complex oscillators generating unstable low-end sub-harmonics.
* Percussion & Texture: Sledgehammers striking damp concrete slabs, scrap-iron sheets hung from chains, plate reverbs fed with pure room-bleed and whispered liturgies.

For Fans Of: Earth, Sunn O))), Swans, Godspeed You! Black Emperor (subverted into sheer sub-bass)

