Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Who Moved the Tripoint Stone?
- Story & Context: Brother Paul woke before dawn in the damp refectory of the Jura Abbey to discover the surveyor's granite boundary marker dragged six meters toward the French ravine. His modular patch rig, coiled like ivy around the altar, had caught the seismic displacement as an involuntary spike of square-wave sub-bass while he slept. The salt on his lips tasted of sulfur when border guards pounded against the reinforced cedar gates, their boots scattering freshly fallen frost. As the first monolithic wall of bass fuzz groaned from the cistern beneath their boots, Paul leaned into the room mic and chanted: "Mark the earth where the sleepers drift away."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: High-contrast monochrome shot of an ancient mossy granite boundary marker tilted on a foggy border trench, surrounded by heavy patch cables rooted into the soil, with microscopic chalk dust hanging in cold winter air.

Track 2: Brother Julian at the Undivided Skerry
- Story & Context: Exiled to an abandoned maritime watchtower on a barren tidal outcrop, Brother Julian spent four winter months patching his recurring sleep-paralysis episodes into an ungrounded 1970s Serge oscillator rack. The damp Atlantic spray had calcified his bass cabinet’s paper cones into white crusts, making every sustained low-C rattle like falling masonry. When an archdiocesan inspector arrived to confiscate the voltage logs, Julian refused to stand, bowing his forehead against the chilled concrete floor while the bowed contrabass feedback vibrated the salt out of the stone joints. He wept into the talkback loop, murmuring into the drone: "Every border is a wound that sings in the deep."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A brutally minimal seaside concrete bunker lashed by dark ocean tides at dusk, illuminated from within by the dull amber glow of vacuum tubes, with salt crystals encrusting thick bass strings laid across a slate altar.

Track 3: The Concrete Map Bleeds Chalk
- Story & Context: Decades after the Jura abbey was decommissioned by the military, cartographers discovered that the subterranean drainage tunnels were still resonating with a persistent, low-frequency hum. Trekkers exploring the derelict bunker found that striking a crowbar against the foundation re-ignited a self-oscillating bass loop permanently imprinted onto the tape reels by the founders' final night of collective dreaming. The surrounding topography had warped under the vibrational load, causing the survey lines to dissolve into white lime seeping across the damp rock face. Scratched across the brutalist altar was the movement's surviving, enigmatic epitaph: "The boundary dissolves when the foundation groans."
